#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Slack, as an agent is reached on it.

Everything about this platform is in this file and nowhere else. A channel id, a `ts`, a
`thread_ts`, a Socket Mode envelope, an agent session and the four-thousand-character message limit
are all Slack's, and none of them appears anywhere under `src/rundesk/`. Nothing there imports
`slack_sdk`, and nothing here imports rundesk. The seam is a pipe carrying newline-delimited JSON,
and it is what lets a product whose own code imports nothing outside the standard library reach a
platform that can only be reached over a websocket.

**A channel is a connection, not a place.** One Slack App per agent, installed into one workspace.
The bot answers direct messages sent to its App Home and it answers a mention in any channel it has
been invited to — with nothing per-place configured here, and with who may reach the agent decided
entirely on rundesk's side.

## When it wakes, and only then

    a direct message                     -> answered where it was said         (no mention needed)
    a mention at the top of a channel    -> answered in a new thread under it
    a mention inside somebody's thread   -> answered in that thread, with bounded prior context
    anything else said in a channel      -> nothing at all

**A thread the agent has already answered in does not wake it again.** That is not a rule this file
applies; it is the shape of what it subscribes to. Only `app_mention` and `message.im` are asked for,
so an unmentioned message in a channel or in a thread produces no event of any kind — there is
nothing to receive, nothing to filter, and nothing that can be got wrong later. It is why several
separately installed agent bots can stand in one thread and each answer only when it is named.

## Deliberately quiet

**The final answer is the only thing this posts.** No running commentary, no tool or activity lines,
no delegation notices, no thinking, no token counts, no timing, and no footer under the answer. The
whole of what a turn looks like here is three things: 👀 on the exact message that asked, Slack's own
agent-session status while the work runs, and ✅ on that message once the answer has gone out.
`activity`, `delegation` and the `cost` a delivery carries are read and deliberately rendered as
nothing — see `_told`, which says so at each of them. Correctness never degrades; only fidelity does,
and here that is the point rather than a shortfall.

## What is rundesk's and is never decided here

**Whether somebody may be answered is rundesk's.** `RUNDESK_ALLOW` and `RUNDESK_ALLOW_PLACES` are
read here only to avoid working for nothing: fetching a thread, or reacting, for somebody who can
never be answered is waste at best and, in a channel full of people, an agent visibly attending to a
stranger it is about to ignore. A message from anybody else is dropped in silence, because replying
to a stranger to say they are a stranger confirms the agent is listening.

**Rundesk decides what state a turn is in; this decides only how that looks.**

**Turn-answer splitting is rundesk's**, in one place, for every platform. `--check` reports
`max_text` in its settings, rundesk splits to it, and the text handed to `deliver` is checked here
and refused if it is past it rather than quietly cut a second time.

**Which interpreter runs this is rundesk's.** It arrives on `PATH` — see `channels/adapters.py`.
`slack_sdk` is imported by name, lazily, and is never located: an adapter that went looking for the
install's own virtualenv is the defect that survived a whole release in the build this replaces.
Its *built-in* Socket Mode client is used rather than the aiohttp, websockets or websocket-client
variants, because that one implements the protocol itself and pulls in nothing further — `slack_sdk`
is one pinned package with no dependency behind it.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import contextlib
import json
import math
import os
import signal
import sys
import threading
import time
import traceback
from typing import Any, Dict, List, NamedTuple, Optional, Set, Tuple

#: The vendor library, bound by `the_library()` at the moment it is first needed.
#:
#: **Never imported at module scope**, because `--capabilities` must answer on a machine where
#: nothing is installed: it is asked before a channel exists, offline, and an adapter that cannot say
#: what it can do until its dependency is present is one rundesk cannot ask.
slack_sdk = None
SocketModeClient = None
SocketModeResponse = None
WebClient = None
SlackApiError = None

#: What Slack takes in one message. **Under its own recommendation on purpose**: Slack advises four
#: thousand characters and truncates hard at forty thousand, and the room between the two is what
#: pays for escaping — `&` becomes `&amp;` on the way out, so text checked at this number cannot
#: reach the hard limit however much of it is reserved punctuation.
MAX_TEXT = 3800

#: What this can do, asked with no network and no account, and the same answer every time.
#:
#: **Honest about being quiet.** Nothing here streams progress and nothing here edits a message it
#: has posted, so both say so rather than claiming an ability the surface deliberately does not use.
#: `react` and `thread` are real: a turn is marked on the message that asked for it, and a mention in
#: a channel is answered in a thread of its own.
#:
#: `attach` is `False` and that is a stated absence rather than an oversight — this adapter fetches
#: no file in and sends no file out, and a delivery carrying one is refused in words that say so.
#:
#: `max_text` is the constant above and never a second copy of it.
#: **`address` is what lets a schedule report somewhere of its own.** rundesk holds no Slack
#: credential, so it cannot turn a user id into the direct conversation that user reads — that is
#: `conversations.open`, a call only this side can make — and the string this adapter composes for a
#: place is its own and never rundesk's to build. Saying so is what allows rundesk to accept `--to`
#: for this channel; an adapter that says nothing has `--to` refused where somebody typed it.
CAPABILITIES = {"stream": False, "edit": "none", "react": True, "thread": True, "attach": False,
                "address": True, "max_text": MAX_TEXT}

